-- lib/kernel.cub — the smallest machine, written in the language (part of machine-in-hyperactive).
-- The paper's §4.5 for functions and sharing only: a wire is a slot that is empty until the first
-- side arrives; a value is a closure (a body and the wires of its environment), a number, or a
-- reference to a held wire; an operation is apply, increment, duplicate, erase, or forward (a wire
-- joined to a wire). sm-rule is every rule: it is given the two cells that met and the first unused
-- wire name, and returns what to put on which wires; it reads nothing else. sm-do is the memory: a
-- value or an operation arriving at a wire, and a read of a held wire. sm-step is the executor's
-- choice of which ready pair goes next (here the first); it is the only place a choice is made.
-- Not here: neutrals (a number applied, or a closure incremented, is dropped), dimensions, systems,
-- composition, data; held wires are never freed. Nothing in it is proven yet.
import "core.cub"

